<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:windows">
<!--
    kuna calleescratchbody: a large-frame MSVC prologue loses its whole stack
    allocation across the out-of-line stack probe, and every local is then
    indexed off a value the function never assigns.

    x86-64-win.cspec lists RAX in <killedbycall> for the default __fastcall, so
    Heritage::guardCalls (heritage.cc:1444) plants an INDIRECT creation of RAX at
    every call.  That is an answer about the CONVENTION.  __chkstk contradicts
    it: it READS RAX (the frame size the caller loaded) and never writes it,
    which is the entire point of the calling sequence
    "mov eax,SIZE; call __chkstk; sub rsp,rax".

    calleepreserves is the pass that narrows killedbycall from the callee's own
    body, and its probe already proves RAX untouched here.  What declined was its
    positive-evidence gate, which asks the callee to have written a register the
    convention promises is PRESERVED - the signature of the i386 get-PC thunk it
    was written for.  __chkstk writes RSP, R10 and R11, and this cspec names none
    of them in either <unaffected> or <killedbycall>: they are scratch, and
    clobbering scratch is not a departure from the convention.

    f, at 0x1000, is the calling sequence reduced to the seam:

        1000  b8 00 20 00 00        MOV   EAX,0x2000
        1005  e8 f6 00 00 00        CALL  0x1100        ; __chkstk
        100a  48 29 c4              SUB   RSP,RAX
        100d  48 89 4c 24 08        MOV   [RSP+0x8],RCX
        1012  48 8b 44 24 08        MOV   RAX,[RSP+0x8]
        1017  48 81 c4 00 20 00 00  ADD   RSP,0x2000
        101e  c3                    RET

    and 0x1100 is MSVC's __chkstk verbatim, spill slot and probe loop included:

        1100  48 83 ec 10           SUB   RSP,0x10
        1104  4c 89 14 24           MOV   [RSP],R10     ; the save that makes it
        1108  4c 89 5c 24 08        MOV   [RSP+0x8],R11 ; safe to clobber R10/R11
        110d  4d 33 db              XOR   R11,R11
        1110  4c 8d 54 24 18        LEA   R10,[RSP+0x18]
        1115  4c 2b d0              SUB   R10,RAX       ; reads RAX
        1118  4d 0f 42 d3           CMOVC R10,R11
        111c  65 4c 8b 1c 25 ..     MOV   R11,GS:[0x10]
        1125  4d 3b d3              CMP   R10,R11
        1128  73 16                 JNC   0x1140
        112a  66 41 81 e2 00 f0     AND   R10W,0xf000
        1130  4d 8d 9b 00 f0 ff ff  LEA   R11,[R11-0x1000]
        1137  41 c6 03 00           MOV   byte ptr [R11],0x0
        113b  4d 3b d3              CMP   R10,R11
        113e  75 f0                 JNZ   0x1130
        1140  4c 8b 14 24           MOV   R10,[RSP]
        1144  4c 8b 5c 24 08        MOV   R11,[RSP+0x8]
        1149  48 83 c4 10           ADD   RSP,0x10
        114d  c3                    RET

    g, at 0x1400, is the NEGATIVE control and has f's shape byte for byte, except
    that its callee at 0x1500 is "endbr64; ret" - a stub, which is also what a
    placeholder and an entry decoded at the wrong address look like.  It writes
    no memory, so its silence about RAX is not evidence and g must render
    identically in both passes.

    Stage: P3 heritage call guarding, option calleescratchbody off|on.  Pass 1
    (off) pins the bug, pass 2 (on, the default) pins the fix.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x1000">
b800200000e8f60000004829c448894c2408488b4424084881c400200000c3
</bytechunk>
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x1100">
4883ec104c8914244c895c24084d33db4c8d5424184c2bd04d0f42d3654c8b1c25100000004d3bd37316664181e200f04d8d9b00f0ffff41c603004d3bd375f04c8b14244c8b5c24084883c410c3
</bytechunk>
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x1400">
b800200000e8f60000004829c448894c2408488b4424084881c400200000c3
</bytechunk>
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x1500">
f30f1efac3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x1000" name="f"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x1400" name="g"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>option calleescratchbody off</com>
  <com>lo fu f</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>lo fu g</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option calleescratchbody on</com>
  <com>lo fu f</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>lo fu g</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="CALLEESCRATCHBODY #1: off, the whole frame is indexed off the value __chkstk supposedly returns" min="1" max="1">Stack0000000000000008\)\[-sub_1100\(\)\] = a0;$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="CALLEESCRATCHBODY #2: on, the probe is called for its side effect and the frame slot is a fixed offset" min="1" max="1">^  sub_1100\(\);$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="CALLEESCRATCHBODY #3: on, the slot written and read back at that offset is the argument" min="1" max="1">^  return a0;$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="CALLEESCRATCHBODY #4: both passes, an endbr64/ret stub writes no memory and proves nothing" min="2" max="2">Stack0000000000000008\)\[-sub_1500\(\)\] = a0;$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="CALLEESCRATCHBODY #5: both passes, so g keeps the convention's answer unchanged" min="2" max="2">^unsigned long long g\(unsigned long long a0\)$</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
